<reference_puzzle index="1" source="eval" puzzle_id="247ef758" lines="35-46">
<description>
Move colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.

Input structure:
- bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.

Rule:
- For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.
- Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.

Key insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="eval" puzzle_id="aa4ec2a5" lines="52-66">
<description>
Add red outlines around objects, and mark enclosed holes inside hollow objects.

Input structure:
- A mostly uniform background contains one or more 4-connected foreground objects.
- Some objects may enclose 4-connected background holes inside their bbox.

Rule:
- For every foreground component, draw a one-cell 8-neighborhood outline in red (2).
- If a component has enclosed background holes, recolor the component to teal (8).
- Fill those enclosed holes with magenta (6) before drawing the outline.

Key insight: Holes are treated as part of the object for outlining, while only objects
that actually enclose holes get the teal/magenta interior recoloring.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="eval" puzzle_id="f560132c" lines="259-273">
<description>
Assemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Several monochrome non-bg sprites (the "pieces"), scattered across the grid.
- One sprite is the legend: it contains a multi-color k×k patch (typically 2×2) encoding output colors.

Rule:
- Tile all pieces (rotations/reflections allowed) into a tight rectangle with no gaps.
- The legend's k×k color patch defines a spatial color map over the rectangle.
- Each piece is recolored to the legend-map color corresponding to its position in the assembled rectangle.

Key insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="a32d8b75" lines="262-275">
<description>
Assemble and place enlarged sprites on a canvas using legend-panel instructions.

Input structure:
- Color-6 separators divide the grid into a left legend column and a main canvas.
- Each legend panel has (top to bottom): a unit sprite, a frame template, a 5x5 bordered square with a position-marker pixel, and a 3x3 rotation clock-hand.

Rule:
- Tile the unit sprite into each active cell of the frame to build an enlarged composite.
- Rotate the composite per the clock-hand direction (up=0, right=90, down=180, left=270 degrees).
- Place it on the canvas anchored at the position encoded by the marker pixel's row/col in the bordered square's 3x3 inner grid.

Key insight: The marker pixel's position in a 3x3 grid jointly encodes vertical and horizontal anchor alignment (start/middle/end).
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="eval" puzzle_id="16b78196" lines="6-20">
<description>
Assemble colored sprites into a central bar's notches, chaining successive pieces along fixed-width strips.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Largest monochrome component is a solid "bar" with boundary notches (bg holes opening to its bbox edge).
- Several smaller colored sprites scattered around the bar.

Rule:
- Each bar notch is filled by a root piece whose shape exactly covers the hole.
- The root defines a strip (column-span or row-span) for its chain.
- Successive pieces fill the bg cells inside the previous piece's bbox, locked to the same strip width.
- Multiple chains grow independently from different bar notches.

Key insight: The root piece's extent perpendicular to the chain direction locks the strip width for all subsequent pieces in that chain.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="195c6913" lines="37-52">
<description>
Simulate particles along a path with alternating turn direction at collisions.

Input structure:
- Top-left: 2x2 monochrome blocks define a repeating color cycle (left-to-right).
- Isolated single pixels of a cycle color: start markers adjacent to the path.
- A 2x2 monochrome block of a distinct color: end marker.

Rule:
- Each start marker launches a particle moving straight, painting cycle colors in order.
- On hitting a boundary, paint that cell with the end color and turn 90°.
- Turn direction alternates: odd collisions → left (CCW), even → right (CW).
- If the scheduled turn is blocked, the particle stops.

Key insight: The alternating left/right turn rule is the core mechanism; without it the path would be trivial.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="de809cff" lines="86-103">
<description>
Turn black dents in two colored regions into marked opposite-color patches.

Input structure:
- Black background with two large foreground colors.
- The large regions contain small black dents and a few one-cell color specks
  or protrusions along region boundaries.

Rule:
- Locally clean the two foreground regions: isolated specks become black, and
  one-cell protrusions surrounded by the other region take that region's color.
- Each original black dent whose cleaned 3x3 neighborhood is mostly one
  foreground color becomes the center of a 3x3 patch in the opposite color.
- The dent center itself is recolored teal (8).

Key insight: The teal pixels mark the original black defects; the surrounding
3x3 patch uses the other foreground color, after first removing boundary noise.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="142ca369" lines="6-19">
<description>
Trace diagonal rays from triangular arrows, reflecting off colored wall pixels.

Input structure:
- bg grid with monochrome 3-pixel L-shaped "arrows" (2x2 bbox missing one corner) and scattered colored wall pixels.

Rule:
- Each arrow fires a ray diagonally away from its missing corner, painting bg cells in its color.
- A head-on wall hit reverses both axes; a side-touch wall hit flips the touched axis only.
- On any bounce the ray adopts the wall's color and recolors the contact cell.
- Ray stops when it exits the grid or reaches another arrow.

Key insight: Two distinct reflection types (corner vs side-touch) plus color inheritance at each bounce drive the path geometry.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="e3721c99" lines="69-83">
<description>
Recolor grey shapes by matching their hole count to a color legend.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color; grey (5) sprites scattered across the grid.
- Colored legend shapes (non-bg, non-grey, at least 2×2, not touching the outer border) near grid edge,
  each with a distinct number of enclosed holes.

Rule:
- Each colored legend shape defines a mapping: hole count → color.
- For every grey sprite, count its holes (enclosed bg regions not touching the sprite's bounding-box border).
- Recolor the grey sprite to the matching legend color; erase grey distractors with unlisted hole counts.

Key insight: Shape identity is encoded by topology (number of holes), not geometry or size.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="64efde09" lines="68-83">
<description>
Copy colored control-ray ports from one rectangular sprite orientation to every matching transformed sprite.

Input structure:
- bg is the most common color.
- Several dense 2-by-N or N-by-2 rectangular sprites are dihedral transforms of one template.
- Isolated single-pixel control markers sit on rays projected outward from the dominant long edge of some sprites.

Rule:
- For each sprite, the active edge is the long side whose cells have the strongest same-color majority.
- A control marker seen first along an outward ray from an active-edge cell assigns its color to that template cell.
- On every transformed sprite, draw that assigned color outward from the corresponding active-edge cell through bg cells until blocked or out of bounds.

Key insight:
- The marker positions are not absolute; they label cells of a shared rectangular template, then the labels follow each sprite's rotation/reflection.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="eval" puzzle_id="8e5c0c38" lines="9-21">
<description>
Clean each color layer by enforcing vertical mirror symmetry.

Input structure:
- bg = background. Several non-bg colors, each forming a nearly-symmetric pattern about some vertical axis.
- Some pixels in each color are "noise" that breaks the symmetry.

Rule:
- For each non-bg color, find the vertical axis that best explains its pixel layout.
- Remove any pixel that lacks a same-color mirror counterpart across that axis.

Key insight: Each color has its own independent symmetry axis; the task is per-color denoising, not whole-grid symmetry.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="eval" puzzle_id="dbff022c" lines="275-288">
<description>
Fill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.
- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.

Rule:
- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.
- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.
- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.

Key insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="eval" puzzle_id="8f3a5a89" lines="22-36">
<description>
Paint an inner border around the region reachable from a corner marker pixel.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color; wall color (blue) forms barriers dividing the grid.
- A unique single pixel (magenta) sits on one corner — the start marker.
- Some wall-colored pixels are structural (border-touching, enclosing regions); others are decorations.

Rule:
- Flood-fill from the marker corner to find all reachable bg cells, blocked by structural walls.
- Erase wall-colored decorations hidden behind structural walls (not reachable from marker side).
- Paint orange (color 7) on every reachable bg cell adjacent to a wall or grid edge — forming a 1-cell inner border.

Key insight: Only border-connected wall components act as real barriers; interior wall fragments are decorations to be removed.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="14" source="eval" puzzle_id="7c66cb00" lines="9-24">
<description>
Dock two-tone sprites into color-matched horizontal bars with a color swap.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Top area: small two-tone sprites (outer color + inner color).
- Below: horizontal bars spanning most of the row, each with a fill color and border color on edges.

Rule:
- Match each sprite to the bar whose fill color equals the sprite's outer color.
- Place the sprite at its original x-position, aligned to the bar's bottom.
- Recolor: sprite outer → bar border, sprite inner → bar fill.
- Erase original sprites from the top area.

Key insight: Color matching is inverted — the sprite's dominant color identifies its target bar, then gets swapped to the bar's accent color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

